            <p>Dear Students,</p>
<p>Following several questions about the upcoming deadlines and project req=
uirements, I would like to clarify a few points for all teams.</p>
<h3>1. Upcoming deadlines</h3>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>November 18:</strong> Mid-term project presentation (SOTA + PoC)=
 =E2=80=94 10-minute team video.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>November 25:</strong> Mandatory mid-term Q&amp;A session. Prepar=
e substantive questions about other teams' projects.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>December 2:</strong> Phase 2 submission =E2=80=94 revised litera=
ture review, EDA, PoC, updated report, source code (Pull Request), and rebu=
ttal.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Mid-term presentations and Teams channel</h3>
<p>A <strong>dedicated Microsoft Teams channel will be created within the n=
ext few days</strong> for submitting and sharing your presentation videos.<=
/p>
<p>You will be able to review other teams' presentations before the Novembe=
r 25 Q&amp;A session, prepare relevant questions, and share them on the cha=
nnel.</p>
<p>The mid-term video should cover your research problem, SOTA, proposed me=
thodology, current PoC progress, preliminary findings, and next steps.</p>
<p><strong>The mid-term video is not graded as a separate deliverable, but =
it is an important preparation milestone for the assessed Phase 2 project.<=
/strong> Its content directly relates to several Phase 2 grading criteria: =
working PoC (2.1), experimental plan (2.3), and revised state of the art (2=
.5).</p>
<p>The presentation and Q&amp;A are opportunities to identify weaknesses, r=
eceive feedback, and improve your work before the December 2 submission. Yo=
ur responses to the feedback and Q&amp;A questions are also assessed under =
Criterion 2.4.</p>
<p>We also have a mandatory Q&amp;A session, which will be much easier for =
all of us with the videos beforehand; otherwise, we would have to listen to=
 20 presentations at once, which is extremely tiring. So we will have only =
short videos and a discussion by each team.</p>
<p><strong>Therefore, please treat the mid-term presentation seriously, eve=
n though it does not carry a separate score.</strong></p>
<h3>3. Phase 2 report =E2=80=94 templates and length</h3>
<p>You may either extend your Phase 1 report or use the final project repor=
t template, transferring and revising relevant sections. Both approaches ar=
e acceptable.</p>
<p>I recommend <strong>approximately 8 pages of main content</strong>, excl=
uding references and appendices. This is a suggestion, <strong>not a mandat=
ory page limit or grading criterion</strong>.</p>
<p>You may place detailed rebuttals, additional experimental results, and s=
upporting technical information in appendices. However, essential methodolo=
gy, results, and conclusions must appear in the main report.</p>
<p><strong>Quality, clarity, and completeness matter more than the exact nu=
mber of pages.</strong></p>
<h3>4. Rebuttal =E2=80=94 part of Phase 2 assessment</h3>
<p><strong>Criterion 2.4: Rebuttal to supervisors' reviews and Q&amp;A ques=
tions =E2=80=94 3 points.</strong></p>
<p>You are expected to:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Address every comment from your supervisors and every question your team=
 received during Q&amp;A.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Explain whether each point was addressed, rejected with justification, o=
r deferred.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Support your arguments with evidence and relevant literature.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Revise your report accordingly</strong>, clearly indicating wher=
e changes were made.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>You may initially prepare your rebuttal using the Project Proposal Templ=
ate, but the resulting corrections and conclusions must be incorporated int=
o your Phase 2 report.</p>
<p>Simply acknowledging comments is not sufficient.</p>
<h3>5. PoC expectations</h3>
<p>For November 18, present what you have actually completed. Small-scale e=
xperiments and preliminary results are acceptable if their limitations are =
clearly explained.</p>
<p>For the graded Phase 2 submission on December 2, Criterion 2.1 requires =
a working, reproducible PoC producing measurable results on real data.</p>
<h3>6. Survey presentations and tutorials</h3>
<p>The <strong>survey presentation/tutorial (Criterion 5c, 10 points)</stro=
ng> is a separate requirement and has not been replaced by the mid-term pro=
ject video.</p>
<p>It involves presenting an NLP research topic grounded in relevant scient=
ific literature, along with the required quiz and demonstration notebook.</=
p>
<p>Please consult the course schedule for the December presentation session=
s.</p>
<h3>7. Scientific reasoning and professional Data Science</h3>
<p>Evidence-based reasoning, methodological rigour, reproducibility, profes=
sional communication, and critical collaboration with AI are important <str=
ong>learning outcomes of this Master's-level course</strong>.</p>
<p>The rebuttal process is not simply an opportunity to negotiate grades. I=
t is an exercise in evaluating evidence, defending methodological decisions=
, responding constructively to criticism, and improving your work.</p>
<p>I am happy to reconsider scores when convincing evidence demonstrates an=
 assessment error. However, <strong>all corrections must be verified and ap=
plied consistently and fairly across teams</strong>.</p>
<p>Finally, <strong>tutorial attendance is mandatory</strong>. Please rerea=
d the published course rules and grading criteria, as well as our universit=
y rules.</p>
<p>Thank you for your engagement and constructive discussions!</p>
<p>Best regards,<br>Anna Wr=C3=B3blewska</p>=0A=
